{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update.json\",\"summary\":\"Submitted the probe the new trim child question asks for. The harness gains a drift-then-trim case: after the usual prefix it performs three reconnection rounds of nodes 1 and 2 (which the repeated-rounds receipt showed to add one index per round), then lets node 2 take the lead so that the new leader's own recorded accepted index is inflated as well, samples all three nodes as DRIFTTRIM_BEFORE, calls the public trim(None) on that leader inside catch_unwind so a panic is reported as a field rather than aborting the run, emits DRIFTTRIM_TRIM with the trim outcome plus the leader's compacted, accepted and decided indices, and samples all three nodes again as DRIFTTRIM_AFTER. The comparisons require the six-index baseline for every node before the trim, that trim(None) reports no error for a configuration whose six indices have been decided, that the compacted index reaches those six indices, and that the leader's decided index remains covered by the entries it holds afterwards; the declared prediction from the source trace is that the drifted minimum can exceed what the configuration has actually decided, so the documented compaction path either reports an error instead of compacting or compacts at a drifted bound. The registered sources are the trim derivation, the minimum helper, the documented all-decided requirement, MemoryStorage's trim arithmetic, and the sync and accsync accounting that produces the drift; the child question remains unresolved until this receipt, and the parent interpretation is unchanged. Validation passes and execution is requested; incomplete are this receipt's interpretation, the drift's bound and reconfiguration-handoff questions, timers and loss triggers, larger clusters, persistence, and any further independent lead.\"}"}}
